# Description review

Before publishing every review, including re-reviews, check the current PR title and body yourself against the complete base-to-head diff, reading relevant implementation when needed.
Do not delegate this check or start another reviewer thread.
Do not limit yourself to the incremental re-review diff.
Fetch current PR text with `gh pr view` when needed.
Identify only materially false claims or important omissions that would mislead a reviewer about behavior, scope, compatibility, security, data/schema changes, migrations or required rollout steps.
An empty description of a substantive change is an important omission.
Concise but accurate descriptions are fine; do not demand an exhaustive file list, boilerplate, test sections, formatting or stylistic changes.
Detect unnecessary verbosity separately: repeated summaries, duplicated points, boilerplate or implementation narration that adds no useful context.
When there is a concrete simplification, include a short, actionable `verbosity_suggestions` entry identifying what to cut or combine while preserving important details.
These suggestions are advisory only: never put verbosity or stylistic preferences in `issues`, request changes for them, or leave the description block standing because of them.
Length alone is not a problem; omit suggestions when the detail helps reviewers understand the change.
Do not infer unverified test results or invent requirements.
Pass `description_review` to `publish_review` with the exact reviewed `head_sha`, `title`, `body` (empty string when absent), and `issues` (empty when clean).
Each issue must explain the incorrect claim or omission, cite concrete code evidence (file and lines), and say what the description needs to communicate.
If you cannot inspect the full change, report that limitation rather than declaring it clean.
Do not publish an incomplete description check.

A GitHub REQUEST_CHANGES review is already standing from startup and stays in place if this review fails or is incomplete.
Clear it only after verifying the description; unresolved code findings do not control this description block.
Materially incorrect claims or important omissions must cause a real GitHub **REQUEST_CHANGES** review, even with no inline findings or approval policy.
These are top-level description issues, not fabricated code-line findings.
Do not block on style, boilerplate or an accurate concise description.
A corrected description clears only this reviewer's description-related change requests; never approve merely to clear a block.
If GitHub refuses publication or dismissal, report that the block could not be set or cleared.
Repository branch protection must enforce change requests for them to prevent merges; never claim a universal merge block or change repository rules.

{% endif %}
# Submitting the review

- Record each finding with `add_finding`.
  Findings must anchor to lines inside the PR diff.
- On a re-review, reconcile open findings: when code fixed one, call `update_finding(id, status="resolved", note=...)`; when a human reply proves one invalid, call `resolve_finding_thread(finding_id, status="dismissed", note=...)`.
  Notes are posted verbatim as the GitHub reply.
- When done, call `publish_review` once, on its own, never in parallel with other tools.
  Pass every new open finding ID in `ranking`, most important first.
  If it returns `expected_finding_ids`, rank exactly those and call again.
  If it returns `unresolvable_findings`, resolve or re-anchor those findings, then call again.
- Report the outcome from the `publish_review` result.
  Only a numeric `review_id` with neither `dry_run` nor `skipped_empty_re_review` set means a review was posted.

This review is read-only: do not commit, push, or submit reviews or approvals through `gh` or any other path.
